BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Obituary: Patsy Lou Asher
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
DTSTAMP:20260815T203515Z
UID:wrightfuneralhomeobit-2026-08-10-patsy-lou-asher-2026-08-10-allday@lin
 n-county-scraper.local
CATEGORIES:obituary
DESCRIPTION:Patsy Lou Asher\, age 89\, of Brookfield\, passed away Monday\
 , August 10\, 2026\, at Pershing Memorial Hospital in Brookfield. A Celebr
 ation of Patsy’s life will be held Thursday\, August 20\, 2026\, at 2:00
  p.m. at Wright Funeral Home in Brookfield with Bro. Jeremy Blackburn offi
 ciating.  Burial will take place at Park Lawn Cemetery in Brookfield. The 
 family will receive friends from 1:00 p.m. until the time of service at th
 e funeral home. Memorials to Pershing Memorial Auxiliary may be left at or
  mailed to Wright Funeral Home\, P.O. Box 496\, Brookfield\, MO 64628. Pat
 sy was born August 27\, 1936\, in Brookfield\, the daughter of Raymond Mor
 ris and Bessie Mae (Adams) Sims. She was a graduate of Brookfield High Sch
 ool. On September 26\, 1954\, she married Donald B. Asher who preceded her
  in death on August 5\, 1980. A familiar face to many throughout the Brook
 field community\, Patsy worked for many years as a salesclerk at longtime 
 local businesses\, including Tooey's and Mart Drug\, as well as several ot
 her retail businesses in the area. Through her work\, she came to know cou
 ntless people and enjoyed the friendships she made along the way. Patsy wa
 s also a talented seamstress whose abilities were well known throughout th
 e Brookfield area. Over the years\, she sewed for many people in the commu
 nity and generously shared her talents with others. One especially meaning
 ful part of her work was ordering and fitting prosthesis and bras for brea
 st cancer survivors\, providing a service that brought comfort and confide
 nce to many women. Outside of work\, Patsy had a great passion for playing
  golf and enjoyed staying active through water aerobics. She also looked f
 orward to attending Ray Ball Auction with her friends\, where she enjoyed 
 the company\, conversation\, and time spent together.  Above all else\, P
 atsy's greatest joy was her family. She dearly loved her children\, grandc
 hildren\, and great-grandchildren and was always proud to share a story ab
 out them. Her family was never far from her thoughts or conversations\, an
 d the memories and stories she shared will remain a treasured part of her 
 legacy. Patsy is survived by her companion\, Terry Kenley of the home\; tw
 o sons\, Donald Asher and wife\, Brenda\, of Brookfield\, and Mark Asher o
 f Lee's Summit\; six grandchildren\, Bailey Calton and husband\, Dave\, of
  Lawson\, Kirby Asher and wife\, Kellie\, of Harrisburg\, Brandon Asher an
 d wife\, Kellan\, of Oak Park\, California\, Aaron Asher and wife\, Jessic
 a\, of Greenwood\, Tyler Asher and wife\, Amanda\, of Lee's Summit\, and T
 revor Asher and wife\, Abby\, of Lee's Summit\; 13 great-grandchildren\; a
  sister-in-law\, Sylvia Morris of St. Catharine\; brother-in-law and siste
 r-in-law Jim and Sheila Cook of Chillicothe\; and many nieces\, nephews\, 
 and other relatives. She was also preceded in death by her parents\, Raymo
 nd Morris and Bessie (Adams) Sims\; her stepfather\, Banty Sims\; two brot
 hers\, Johnny Morris and Ronald Morris\; a daughter-in-law\, Teresa "Terri
 " Asher\; father-in-law and mother-in-law\, Ted and Viola Asher\;  sisters
 -in-law and brothers-in-law\, Shirley Asher Bohannon Cook\, Eldon Bohannon
 \, and Ronald and Verna Asher. Patsy will be remembered for her independen
 t spirit\, the many talents she shared with her community\, the friendship
 s she made throughout her life\, and\, most importantly\, the tremendous l
 ove and pride she had for her family.\n\nFull obituary and service details
 : https://www.wright-funeralhome.com/obituaries/Patsy-Lou-Asher?obId=49227
 949
LOCATION:Brookfield\, MO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Obituary: Doris Jean Dougherty
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTSTAMP:20260815T203515Z
UID:wrightfuneralhomeobit-2026-08-06-doris-jean-dougherty-2026-08-06-allda
 y@linn-county-scraper.local
CATEGORIES:obituary
DESCRIPTION:Doris Jean Dougherty\, 88\, of Sumner\, Missouri\, where she m
 ade her home for more than 65 years\, passed away peacefully on Thursday\,
  August 6\, 2026. Doris was born on June 6\, 1938\, in Brookfield\, Missou
 ri\, the daughter of Frank and Iva Lee (Slater) Povlovich. She graduated f
 rom Brookfield High School with the Class of 1956. On July 29\, 1956\, she
  married the love of her life\, John Henry Dougherty. Together they moved 
 to Sumner to begin a life centered on faith\, family\, and farming. John p
 receded her in death on September 22\, 2015\, after suffering a stroke whi
 le driving a truck. Following his passing\, Doris continued living on the 
 family farm on Lakeside Drive\, a place she dearly loved. While John farme
 d\, Doris devoted herself to being a homemaker and raising their three chi
 ldren\, Sondra\, Gregory\, and Kurt. She was much more than a homemaker\, 
 however\, as she worked alongside John on the farm by managing the family'
 s financial records and bookkeeping. She took great pride in maintaining a
  large vegetable garden\, strawberry patch\, and beautiful flower beds eac
 h year. She also lovingly cared for the family's pecan grove\, where she h
 arvested\, cracked\, and sold pecans at festivals throughout the area. Dor
 is was a faithful member of the Sumner United Methodist Church. Her church
  family was an important part of her life\, and she generously shared her 
 time by teaching Sunday school\, helping with the after-school program\, d
 riving children to Vacation Bible School\, and helping build the church fl
 oat for the annual Goose Festival Parade. She was also an active member of
  the United Methodist Women and served as a regional librarian for the org
 anization. Always willing to help others succeed\, Doris encouraged many a
 spiring business owners and often taught them how to keep their financial 
 records and books. Doris and John shared a love of travel and treasured ad
 ventures together\, including memorable trips to Alaska and Hawaii\, where
  they made lifelong friends. Their greatest joy\, however\, was making mem
 ories with their family. They enjoyed vacations to Six Flags Over Texas an
 d countless trips to the Lake of the Ozarks\, where their children spent s
 ummers swimming and water skiing. After purchasing a camper\, they enjoyed
  many camping adventures with family and friends. Doris especially loved f
 ishing and found great joy in teaching many of her grandchildren how to fi
 sh\, creating memories they will cherish for a lifetime. Above all else\, 
 Doris treasured her family. Her love\, generosity\, steady guidance\, and 
 servant's heart touched the lives of everyone around her. She leaves behin
 d a legacy of faith\, hard work\, kindness\, and unconditional love that w
 ill continue through the generations she helped shape. She is survived by 
 her three children\, Sondra Wilson of Waverly\, Missouri\, Greg Dougherty 
 of Sumner\, Missouri\, and Kurt Dougherty and wife Becky of Brookfield\, M
 issouri\; six grandchildren\, Laura Hastings\, Karissa Frizzell and husban
 d Jason\, Eric Dougherty and wife Mallory\, Anna Dougherty\, Joshua Vicker
 y and wife Arial\, and Jacob Dougherty\; nine great-grandchildren\, Kaytli
 n Rogers and husband Landon\, Julia Hastings\, Allison Hastings\, Madison 
 Frizzell\, Logan Frizzell\, Eric Dougherty\, Anna Dougherty\, Jolee Doughe
 rty\, and two additional great-grandchildren\; two sisters\, Virginia and 
 Sharon\; one brother\, Joe\; several nieces\, nephews\, and many other ext
 ended family members and friends. She was preceded in death by her parents
 \; her beloved husband\, John Henry Dougherty\; and one brother\, Jimmy. F
 uneral services will be held at 5:30 p.m. on Friday\, August 14\, 2026\, a
 t Wright Funeral Home\, with Pastor Charles Littrell officiating. Visitati
 on will be held from 4:00 p.m. until the time of the service at the funera
 l home. Burial will take place at a later date in Lakeside Cemetery in Sum
 ner\, Missouri. Memorial contributions are suggested to Lakeside Cemetery.
 \n\nFull obituary and service details: https://www.wright-funeralhome.com/
 obituaries/Doris-Jean-Dougherty?obId=49218621
LOCATION:Brookfield\, MO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Obituary: Sharon Stratton
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260804
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260805
DTSTAMP:20260815T203515Z
UID:wrightfuneralhomeobit-2026-08-04-sharon-stratton-2026-08-04-allday@lin
 n-county-scraper.local
CATEGORIES:obituary
DESCRIPTION:Sharon (Collier) Stratton\, age 84\, of Brookfield\, passed aw
 ay peacefully on Tuesday\, August 4\, 2026\, at Stonebridge Senior Living 
 in Chillicothe. A Celebration of Sharon’s Life will be held at a later d
 ate. Memorials to Laclede United Methodist Church may be left at or mailed
  to Wright Funeral Home\, P.O. Box 496\, Brookfield\, MO 64628. Sharon was
  born\, July 10\, 1942\, at her grandmother’s house in Brookfield.  She 
 was the first child to Paul and Grace Collier and always enjoyed being fir
 st.  She lived in Brookfield then moved to Laclede where her father worked
  with Burlington Northern Railroad and her mother worked at Henderson’s 
 then later at the Laclede Post Office.  Everyone around Laclede knew their
  family\, and soon\, in 1945\, her sister\, Karen\, came along.  A little 
 sister to play and have fun with!  Then in 1952\, baby brother\, Clifford 
 Ray\, had two big sisters to deal with.  Growing up was grand! Some troubl
 es came in 1953\, when Polio reared its ugly head and the girls had so man
 y trips to the hospital.  Laclede supported the whole family when they wer
 e struggling\, there was so much love as well. Sharon came out of it okay 
 but was always in the hospital looking out for little sister in those earl
 y days.  Once they were back in school\, there was baseball\, basketball\,
  4-H\, campfire girls\, and so many grandparents\, cousins\, neighbors\, f
 riends and classmates to come home too.  By senior year\, Sharon was at Me
 adville High School and graduated in 1960. By 1962\, she was a married lad
 y with a handsome husband named John Stratton\, that she met during her se
 nior year in Meadville.  She was then on to college where she graduated in
  1964 with a  bachelor’s degree in accounting and business management fr
 om Warrensburg.  She and John later moved to Chillicothe and Sharon starte
 d her career at City Bank\, as an accountant\, while John worked with fami
 ly running a tractor all over the county. Within the next few years Sharon
  became a Marine Corps wife and moved around a lot.  She lived in so many 
 places\, like Independence MO\, Kansas City MO\, Jacksonville NC\, and Nor
 folk VA\, and always found jobs working with numbers.  In Virginia\, she l
 oved to watch the ships in the bay come and go from her office window.  Jo
 hn was busy learning how to be a Marine and eventually serving in Vietnam.
   Life in the Marine Corps eventually brought them to California where Sha
 ron could be close to her beloved cousin\, Jean and all of her family.  Th
 en babies finally came along\, Craig in 1969 and Dona in 1972.  After a fe
 w more years\, jobs and lots of growing\, Sharon settled with her family i
 n Northern California. She became a business owner in Napa and helped seve
 ral generations of families and businesses with all their income tax\, boo
 kkeeping\, and investment needs.  She had a solid family\, a thriving busi
 ness\, and all she needed was a little more to keep her busy\, so she coac
 hed kids ball teams\, taught every child she knew to swim\, and still had 
 time to lead campfire girls and boy scout groups. Sharon and John also enj
 oyed raising dogs and puppies and collecting classic cars.  Then she remem
 bered how much she loved bowling!  She was an amazing bowler\, sponsored t
 eams\, made it into the local hall of fame\, enjoyed many city tournaments
 \, and also ran a local tournament for several years to benefit veterans. 
 Being far away from her Missouri home meant lots of trips to visit.  She a
 lways liked a houseful\, so many people came to live at the dream home she
  loved in Napa.  It got crowded at times\, but that’s how a California f
 amily was built\, and she enjoyed every minute of it! We lost our dear sis
 ter\, daughter\, wife\, mother\, aunt\, cousin and friend to Dementia.  It
  was a long hard battle\, but the care needed brought her back home to Mis
 souri\, where she was cared for by family\, new friends\, old friends\, an
 d a very special family that we found in Napa and somehow ended up with us
  right here in Missouri!  You know who you are\, and we love you. Sharon i
 s survived by numerous loving family members and friends who will cherish 
 her memory. She was preceded in death by beloved family whom she will joyf
 ully reunite with in Heaven. Sharon left this world on Tuesday\, August 4\
 , 2026\, at 8:00 p.m.\, at the age of 84.  She was surrounded by loved one
 s and the gentle hands of staff at Stonebridge Senior Living in Chillicoth
 e\, Three River’s Hospice\, and our home care team.  We are so grateful 
 for their care and for helping her achieve the peace that she was needing.
  Her family would like to thank everyone in Laclede\, Brookfield\, Meadvil
 le and Chillicothe for welcoming her home\, and to the countless caregiver
 s along the way that truly shared their whole hearts.\n\nFull obituary and
  service details: https://www.wright-funeralhome.com/obituaries/Sharon-Str
 atton?obId=49204979
LOCATION:Brookfield\, MO
END:VEVENT
